<header class="sg-head"><p class="featured-region">War and march · c. 1794 · Europe</p><h1 class="sg-title">Men of Harlech</h1><p class="sg-local"><bdi lang="cy">Rhyfelgyrch Gwŷr Harlech</bdi></p><p class="sg-kid" data-say>A proud Welsh march, said to tell of the men who held Harlech Castle through a siege of seven years in the 1460s.</p></header>
<dl class="sg-facts"><div><dt>Words</dt><dd>various: Welsh words by John Jones (Talhaiarn) and by John Ceiriog Hughes; English words by Thomas Oliphant, John Oxenford and others</dd></div><div><dt>Music</dt><dd>traditional</dd></div><div><dt>Culture</dt><dd>Welsh</dd></div><div><dt>Language</dt><dd>Welsh, English</dd></div><div><dt>Place</dt><dd>Wales</dd></div><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>tune first published 1794; first printed with words c. 1830</dd></div><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>War and march</dd></div><div><dt>The words</dt><dd>Public domain (traditional tune, published 1794; Welsh and English words printed from c. 1830 to 1873)</dd></div></dl>
<div class="sg-body">
<section class="sg-sec" id="story"><h2>The story</h2><p>&quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/men-of-harlech">Men of Harlech</a>&quot;, or &quot;The March of the Men of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harlech">Harlech</a>&quot; (in Welsh &quot;Rhyfelgyrch Gwŷr Harlech&quot;), is a song and military march traditionally said to tell of the siege of Harlech Castle from 1461 to 1468, when the castle was held for the Lancastrians against the Yorkists in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wars-of-the-roses">Wars of the Roses</a>. Its garrison, commanded by the constable Dafydd ap Ieuan, son of the Baron of Hendwr, withstood the longest known siege in the history of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-isles">British Isles</a>; &quot;Through Seven Years&quot; is another name for the song. It has also been linked with the earlier, shorter siege of about 1407 to 1409, between the forces of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/owain-glyndwr">Owain Glyndŵr</a> and the future Henry V of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>. The song holds an important place in Welsh national culture.</p><p>The tune was first published, without words, in 1794 as &quot;Gorhoffedd Gwŷr <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harlech">Harlech</a>: March of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/men-of-harlech">Men of Harlech</a>&quot; in the second edition of The Musical and Poetical Relicks of the Welsh Bards, though it is said to be a much older folk song. The earliest version found with words is a broadside printed around 1830. Welsh words first appeared with it in Y Canigydd, edited by the poet John Owen (Owain Alaw) and published in Wrexham in 1860, and a second edition, Gems of Welsh Melody, gave Welsh and English words in Ruthin in 1862. In the same year the collection Welsh Melodies printed it with Welsh words by the poet John Jones (Talhaiarn) and English words by Thomas Oliphant, President of the Madrigal Society, plainly tying it to Glyndŵr's siege. The Welsh words of John Ceiriog Hughes are found in Brinley Richards's The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Songs</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a> (1873), and John Oxenford's English words were published the same year. There have been many more English versions since, and none is accepted as the standard one.</p><p>It is widely used as a regimental march, above all by British and Commonwealth regiments with historic ties to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a>. It is the slow march of the Welsh Guards and the quick march of the Royal Welsh and of the Royal Canadian Mounted Police, and a march of several Canadian regiments. Haydn arranged it, Edward German used it in the final movement of his Welsh Rhapsody (1904), and William Thomas Best wrote an organ fantasia on it. Fans of Welsh football clubs and schools and colleges around the world sing their own words to it, and a comic parody, &quot;Woad&quot; or &quot;National Anthem of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britons-celtic-people">Ancient Britons</a>&quot;, was written by William Hope-Jones some time before 1914.</p><p>The cinema made it known worldwide. It was first heard in films over the titles of How Green Was My Valley (1941), and it is best known from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zulu-kingdom">Zulu</a> (1964), where it is sung with words written for the film, in counterpoint to the Zulu war chants and the pounding of their shields; the film's editor, John Jympson, cut the scene so that the song keeps its place on either side of the cuts. It is also heard in Zulu Dawn, about the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-isandlwana">Battle of Isandlwana</a>, and in The Englishman Who Went up a Hill but Came down a Mountain (1995). The Royal Regiment of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a>' band recorded it in the church at Rorke's Drift, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a>, on the 120th anniversary of the battle there. On 11 September 2001 Rick Rescorla, head of security for Morgan Stanley's offices in the World Trade Center, sang a Cornish adaptation of it through a bullhorn, among other songs, to keep up the spirits of the staff as he led their evacuation; after helping to save more than 2,700 of them he went back into the towers to bring out others, and was killed when they collapsed.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="meaning"><h2>What it says</h2><p>A song of defiance in arms. In its many sets of words the men of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harlech">Harlech</a>, or of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a>, are called to stand and fight for their land against an invader, and to keep its freedom whatever the cost; the march tune gives the words their swing and pride. No one text is the song: the versions differ in their images, but they share the call to arms and the spirit of resistance.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="words"><h2>The words</h2><div class="sg-blocks sg-blocks-2"><div class="sg-lyrics sg-cols-1"><div class="sg-colhead" aria-hidden="true"><span class="sg-o">Welsh</span></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="cy">Wele goelcerth wen yn fflamio</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="cy">A thafodau tân yn bloeddio</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="cy">Ar i'r dewrion ddod i daro,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="cy">Unwaith eto'n un.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="cy">Gan fanllefau'r tywysogion,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="cy">Llais gelynion, trwst arfogion,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="cy">A charlamiad y marchogion,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="cy">Craig ar graig a grŷn.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="cy">Arfon byth ni orfydd,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="cy">Cenir yn dragywydd;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="cy">Cymru fydd fel Cymru fu,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="cy">Yn glodus ym mysg gwledydd.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="cy">'Ngwyn oleuni'r goelcerth acw,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="cy">Tros wefusau Cymro'n marw,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="cy">Annibyniaeth sydd yn galw,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="cy">Am ei dewraf dyn.</span></p></div></div><div class="sg-lyrics sg-cols-1"><div class="sg-colhead" aria-hidden="true"><span class="sg-e">In English</span></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>Hark! I hear the foe advancing,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>Barbed steeds are proudly prancing,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>Helmets in the sunbeams glancing</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>Glitter through the trees.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>Men of Harlech, lie ye dreaming?</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>See ye not their falchions gleaming,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>While their pennons gaily streaming</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>Flutter in the breeze?</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>From the rocks rebounding,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>Let the war cry sounding</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>Summon all at Cambria's call,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>The haughty foe surrounding,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>Men of Harlech, on to glory!</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>See, your banner famed in story</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>Waves these burning words before ye</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>&quot;Britain scorns to yield!&quot;</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>'Mid the fray, see dead and dying,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>Friend and foe together lying;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>All around, the arrows flying,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>Scatter sudden death!</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>Frighten'd steeds are wildly neighing,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>Brazen trumpets hoarsely braying,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>Wounded men for mercy praying</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>With their parting breath!</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>See! they're in disorder!</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>Comrades, keep close order!</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>Ever they shall rue the day</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>They ventured o'er the border!</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>Now the Saxon flies before us!</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>Vict'ry's banner floateth o'er us!</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>Raise the loud exulting chorus</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>&quot;Britain wins the field,&quot;</span></p></div></div></div><div class="sg-credit"><p>Original: John Jones (Talhaiarn), Welsh Melodies (1862). Text from <a href="https://cy.wikisource.org/wiki/Rhyfelgyrch_Gw%C5%B7r_Harlech_(1)" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a>; public domain.</p><p>English: Thomas Oliphant, Welsh Melodies (1862). Text from <a href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Men_of_Harlech_(Oliphant%2C_1862)" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a>; public domain.</p></div></section>
</div>
<section class="sg-sec" id="listen"><h2>Listen</h2><div class="sg-video" data-yt="0PhxMNinVuo" data-title="Men of Harlech: Bryn Terfel, bass-baritone (he recorded it for We'll Keep a Welcome, 2000)"><button type="button" class="sg-play"><span class="sg-play-ic" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="sg-play-t">Play the performance</span><span class="sg-play-n">Bryn Terfel, bass-baritone (he recorded it for We'll Keep a Welcome, 2000)</span></button></div><p class="sg-video-cr">On YouTube, from the channel Bryn Terfel - Topic. Pressing play loads YouTube&#8217;s player. <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=0PhxMNinVuo" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Watch on YouTube<span aria-hidden="true"> &#8599;</span></a></p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="inspired"><h2>What it inspired</h2><ul class="sg-chain"><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Welsh Melodies, vol. II (Talhaiarn and Thomas Oliphant)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1862</span><span class="sg-chain-h">Welsh and English words</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">The Songs of Wales (Welsh words by John Ceiriog Hughes)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1873</span><span class="sg-chain-h">new Welsh words</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">John Oxenford's English words</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1873</span><span class="sg-chain-h">new English words</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Arrangement by Franz Joseph Haydn, Hob. XXXIb:2</span><span class="sg-chain-h">arrangement</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Welsh Rhapsody (Edward German)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1904</span><span class="sg-chain-h">used in the final movement</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Fantasia for organ (William Thomas Best)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">fantasia</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Woad, or National Anthem of the Ancient Britons (William Hope-Jones)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">before 1914</span><span class="sg-chain-h">parody</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">How Green Was My Valley (film)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1941</span><span class="sg-chain-h">heard over the titles</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Zulu (film; words by John Barry Prendergast)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1964</span><span class="sg-chain-h">new words for a film</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">We'll Keep a Welcome (Bryn Terfel)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">2000</span><span class="sg-chain-h">recording</span></li></ul></section>
